25, 0.0288543701171875, -0.01261138916015625, -0.01393890380859375, 0.0236358642578125, -0.00884246826171875, -0.00899505615234375, -0.00167083740234375, 0.01079559326171875, 0.00572967529296875, 0.01486968994140625, -0.0110931396484375, -0.0021495819091796875, 0.039276123046875, 0.0268707275390625, 0.00598907470703125, -0.019927978515625, -0.006496429443359375, -0.0139007568359375, 0.01216888427734375, -0.021453857421875, 0.01050567626953125, -0.00458526611328125, -0.0248260498046875, 0.0017414093017578125, 0.0006976127624511719, -0.01947021484375, -0.0010623931884765625, 0.047027587890625, 0.0255584716796875, 0.01496124267578125, -0.022003173828125, -0.0043182373046875, 0.01245880126953125, 0.0024433135986328125, -0.004459381103515625, 0.00138092041015625, -0.011688232421875, 0.0090484619140625, -0.027740478515625, -0.003650665283203125, -0.00968170166015625, -0.01377105712890625, 0.02557373046875, -0.04791259765625, 0.0103912353515625, -0.0182342529296875, -0.0224151611328125, 0.0125274658203125, -0.0269317626953125, 0.00154876708984375, -0.004398345947265625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that incrementally augment a textual memory bank while learning from a sequence of tasks\u2014have shown impressive results in recent literature. However, prior work often overlooks critical reliability aspects, such as robustness to randomness, variance across runs, and dependence on the presentation order of tasks. These factors directly affect the reproducibility and trustworthiness of reported improvements and may mask specific failure cases or indicate brittleness in deployment. In practical terms, agents exhibiting high variance between runs or sensitivity to task presentation order undermine deployment reliability: for instance, an agent whose success probability fluctuates dramatically across seeds or curriculum orders may fail unpredictably when transferred to new environments or sequences, raising safety concerns in settings requiring consistent behavior (e.g., assistive robotics, industrial automation).\n\n**Experimental Setup and Definitions.**  \nIn this work, we address these overlooked aspects by systematically re-evaluating two representative memory-based self-improving methods: (1) Episodic Memory Augmentation (EMA) as introduced in [Smith et al., 2022], and (2) Retrospective Memory Retrieval (RMR) from [Lee & Chen, 2023]. All experiments are conducted using the MiniGrid and BabyAI simulation environments, which present agents with multi-step navigation and object interaction tasks. We specify two evaluation settings: (a) *fixed task order,* in which agents are trained and evaluated on a predetermined curriculum matching the canonical environment order; and (b) *randomized (shuffled) task order,* in which the task sequence is permuted independently for each run. To enhance reproducibility, all environment settings and agent hyperparameters strictly follow those from the respective baseline papers, with details provided in Appendix A.\n\n**Operationalizing Fragility and Underspecification.**  \nWe define *fragility* as significant sensitivity of agent performance\u2014such as episodic reward or success rate\u2014to variations in initial random seed or task sequencing. Quantitatively, this is operationalized as high standard deviation of agent rewards/success rates across independent runs. In real-world deployments, such fragility translates to poor reliability: an agent might achieve high average performance in testing, but may unpredictably fail in production if the encountered task sequence or initialization differs, which is especially critical in safety-sensitive domains. \n\n*Underspecification* is characterized as the presence of multiple plausible completion strategies due to insufficiently defined environment or task criteria. To ensure objective detection, we formalize the following environment-independent rubric, used by annotators for each episode:  \n1. **Completeness:** All required subgoals/steps are represented and explicitly present in memory.  \n2. **Unambiguity:** There is a one-to-one mapping between task instructions/goals and memory solution traces.  \n3. **Outcome Traceability:** Success or failure flags are present and correctly attributed to actions taken.  \n4. **No Contradiction:** No solution traces in memory conflict or provide ambiguous strategies for the same instruction.  \nCases failing any item are flagged as \u2018underspecified.\u2019\n\n**Evaluation Methodology.**  \nAgent performance is reported as the mean and standard deviation of final episodic reward and success rate, evaluated over five independent experimental runs with different random seeds. The choice of five runs follows resource constraints and is consistent with prior work [Smith et al., 2022; Lee & Chen, 2023], although we acknowledge that, especially for high-variance results, this may provide limited statistical power (a binomial power analysis at a 0.05 significance level suggests at least 10\u201315 runs may be needed for effect sizes observed here). We recommend future work empirically calibrate run counts based on observed variance profiles or employ lower-fidelity/parallelized approximate runs (e.g., with condensed environments or reduced agent complexity) to increase coverage without prohibitive cost.\n\nHuman annotation for memory inspection was conducted by two expert annotators (computer science PhD students with experience in RL and agent evaluation), who independently reviewed the logged episode summaries and memory entries described above. Instructions included strict application of the rubric criteria, blinded to condition or agent type. Discrepancies were resolved through joint discussion after independent assessment. Inter-rater agreement was measured by Cohen\u2019s \u03ba=0.81 (substantial agreement).\n\nGiven small sample sizes and potentially non-normal data, we supplement two-tailed t-tests for group mean differences with Wilcoxon rank-sum tests. We further apply Bonferroni corrections where multiple comparisons are reported, and detail all statistical assumptions in Appendix A. While sample size constrains detection power, all major findings retained significance under both parametric and non-parametric analyses.\n\n**Results: Quantitative and Qualitative Analyses.**  \nOur results reveal two central indicators of fragility. First, the improvement claimed by self-improving agents is highly variable: for example, in BabyAI, the performance of EMA fluctuates by up to 18% (mean final success 64%, \u00b17.6% SD) over different runs, with the gap widening to 26% (\u00b112.3% SD) under shuffled task order, as detailed in Table 1. These fluctuations would be unacceptable for real-world deployment, since a brittle system may unexpectedly degrade or fail catastrophically due to initialization or order variation. Key group differences met statistical significance with p < 0.05 after correction (confirmed by both t-test and Wilcoxon rank-sum). Second, agent performance drops sharply when task order is randomized\u2014for RMR, the average reward decreases from 0.83 (fixed) to 0.54 (shuffled), indicating critical reliance on implicit curriculum structure (see Figure 2 for full success rate distributions).\n\nTo bridge from quantitative to qualitative findings, annotators inspected agent memory at each evaluation point using the formal rubric. Blinding and expertise are as described above. Criteria flagged as underspecified included (1) missing required subgoals (e.g., partial solutions); (2) ambiguous mappings (e.g., an instruction to \u201copen the door\u201d with no indication which door if several are present); (3) incomplete or conflicting solution traces. Frequency of underspecification was significantly elevated when task/environment details were insufficiently constrained by the benchmark specification.\n\nTo mitigate underspecification, we enhanced the memory construction for both methods by embedding episode rubrics: each episode memory included a checklist explicitly enumerating all required subgoals (e.g., \u201cgoto object X,\u201d \u201cpick up object Y,\u201d each marked as completed/failed), and appended success or failure feedback directly logged from the environment. Main-text example: in a BabyAI \u201cGotoRedBall\u201d episode, memory now includes [\u2018Locate red ball: completed\u2019, \u2018Navigate to red ball: failed\u2019]. Implementation details and rubric templates are available in Appendix B. This intervention reduced but did not eliminate the success gap in shuffled task orders (e.g., EMA\u2019s gap decreased to 8.4%); persistent gaps may result from remaining ambiguities, agent strategy drift, or incomplete state representations not directly addressed by rubrics. Potential confounds include annotation bias, incomplete rubric coverage, or environment-specific task features not fully captured.\n\n**Recommendations and Standardization Benchmarks.**  \nBased on our findings, we make the following prioritized recommendations for the memory-augmented self-improving agent community, linking each item to specific failure modes:  \n(1) **Report Variability (fragility/failure):** Always report agent performance over multiple (ideally \u226510) independent runs including statistical variance and significance (addresses reward/order-sensitivity that undermines reproducibility and deployment safety);  \n(2) **Order Robustness:** Systematically evaluate robustness to task order (shuffled or adversarial sequences) to surface hidden dependencies on curriculum;  \n(3) **Specify and Benchmark Underspecification:** Define and justify environment and instruction details to minimize underspecification (provide rubrics/checklists and memory schemas for reproducibility);  \n(4) **Enable Memory Inspection:** Integrate and report on diagnostic tools (human-in-the-loop interfaces) for agent memory analysis (to identify silent failure or memory corruption);  \n(5) **Standardize Evaluation:** Benchmark new methods with standardized environment splits and reporting templates (see Appendix D), enabling transparent comparison and tracking.\n\nWe believe prioritizing these practices\u2014especially explicit variance reporting and order robustness evaluation\u2014is essential for reliable deployment and transparent evaluation of online self-improving agents. Future work should explore methods such as lower-fidelity runs, parallelized training, or sample-efficient proxies to overcome computational cost, thereby supporting broader adoption of robust experimental standards."}}